# Phase 3 channel-event admission mapping — 20260928T062003Z

- Compared Java 5.5.1 `NettyRemotingAbstract.putNettyEvent`, `NettyEventExecutor.run`, `NettyRemotingClient.shutdown`, and `ServiceThread.shutdown`: Java's unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` admits while the pre-insert size is `<= 10000` (101st? no: maximum 10001), and its worker polls at most one event per loop while not stopped. Shutdown does not guarantee draining queued entries.
- Replaced Rust's Tokio bounded queue (capacity 10001) with an unbounded channel and the Java-style pre-insert `len <= 10000` drop check. This intentionally preserves Java's check-then-insert concurrency race rather than claiming a strict globally bounded capacity; queue can exceed 10001 with racing producers, as the Java queue can. Accepted enqueue order is FIFO.
- Added `channel_event_queue_accepts_10001_then_drops_overflow_fifo`, exercising concurrent producers, per-producer FIFO, the 10001st admission, and post-threshold rejection via the actual enqueue helper.
- Updated `PHASE_3_REMOTING_INVENTORY.md`, ADR-0001 and `JAVA_5_5_1_COMPATIBILITY.md`: document admission, FIFO, shutdown/drop limits, Tokio scheduling difference, and clarify that PARTIAL status requires explicit disposition and never implicitly waives missing functionality.
- Focused test/gates and full diff review not yet completed. Phase 3 remains unaccepted; remaining inventory dispositions, bilingual plan verification, full local gates and final audit are still required. P3-V04 remains pending Phase 19. No commit made in this session; historical untracked rlog `20260927T085045Z.rlog` left untouched.
- Correction to the shorthand above: 10001 is the maximum sequential admission at a stable queue length; it is not an "101st" event. With concurrent producers, both implementations can exceed 10001 because the pre-insert size check and insertion are not atomic. Rust uses an atomic queue-length counter only to reproduce that check-then-insert race shape; the Tokio channel itself remains unbounded. The counter decrements on dequeue, before listener callback execution, matching Java queue size semantics.
- Java source verified directly in `remoting/.../NettyRemotingAbstract.java` (`putNettyEvent` and `NettyEventExecutor.run`), `remoting/.../NettyRemotingClient.java` (`shutdown`), and `remoting/.../common/ServiceThread.java` (`shutdown` default non-interrupt and bounded join). The Java worker's loop checks stopped before each timed poll; queued entries can remain on shutdown.
